== Foreword
<foreword>
Silver occupies an interesting middle position in classical Indian
metallurgy. It is structurally similar to gold (both noble metals, both
relatively inert, both used in lost-wax casting, both incorporated in
panchaloha) but chemically more reactive — silver tarnishes in air,
reacts with sulfur compounds, and forms colored compounds far more
readily than gold. This greater reactivity is encoded in the tradition:
silver’s #emph[graha] is #strong[Chandra] (Moon), associated with
#emph[manas] (mind, emotional fluctuation, receptivity to influence),
while gold’s Surya is associated with stable centrality.

The chemical consequence shows up clearly in the bhasma form. Where
#emph[swarna-bhasma] is essentially pure FCC nano-gold (Volume II §2.4),
#emph[rajata-bhasma] is #strong[silver sulfide] (Ag₂S, acanthite) with
intermediate Ag-Hg-S phases. The classical preparation method
incorporates mercury and sulfur; for gold, these co-mediators leave
during processing and the final product is metallic gold. For silver,
the co-mediators #emph[react] with the silver, and the final product is
a metal-sulfur compound rather than pure silver.

This is not a deficiency of either substance or procedure — it is what
the chemistry permits. Silver’s reactivity with sulfur (the same
property that makes silverware tarnish black in the presence of egg
yolks) means classical processing forms Ag₂S as a stable end-state. The
tradition’s claim that this Ag₂S form has therapeutic value is supported
by modern research: silver sulfide nanoparticles in the 10-100 nm range
have demonstrated antimicrobial activity, anticancer effects (IC50 78.54
μg/ml against MCF-7 breast cancer cells), and excellent biocompatibility
owing to silver’s noble-metal status.

This volume covers silver’s identity, classical preparation, modern
characterization, framework timing predictions, and the empirical
convergence findings — which differ from gold’s in instructive ways.

