== 1. Identity
<identity>
=== 1.1 Substance
<substance>
Silver is the chemical element with symbol Ag (from Latin
#emph[argentum]) and atomic number 47. Among the noble metals, silver is
significantly more reactive than gold:

- #strong[Reactivity with sulfur]: silver reacts with H₂S, H₂SO₄,
  sulfur-containing organics to form silver sulfide (Ag₂S, black
  tarnish) at room temperature
- #strong[Reactivity with halides]: silver dissolves in nitric acid and
  in concentrated sulfuric; reacts with halide salts to form sparingly
  soluble silver halides (the basis of photographic emulsion chemistry)
- #strong[Inertness to oxygen]: silver does NOT directly react with O₂
  in dry air (unlike copper or iron); but in the presence of trace H₂S,
  tarnishes to Ag₂S

Physical properties: - Density: 10.49 g/cm³ at 20°C (lighter than gold,
denser than copper) - Melting point: 961.78°C (lower than gold’s 1064°C;
same range for #emph[puta] heating) - Color: distinctive bright
white-grey metallic luster - Malleability and ductility: extreme —
second only to gold among metals - Hardness: 2.5-2.7 Mohs (slightly
harder than gold) - Highest electrical and thermal conductivity of any
metal - FCC (face-centered cubic) crystal structure, lattice constant
4.0853 Å (very close to gold’s 4.0786 Å — silver and gold form
continuous solid solutions)

The high electrical/thermal conductivity is significant for one of the
classical claims: silver is described as exceptionally responsive to
environmental influences, which the tradition encodes as its association
with the Moon (Chandra, ruler of mind/manas).

=== 1.2 Nomenclature
<nomenclature>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] and the #emph[Charaka Samhita], silver’s
Sanskrit names include:

- #strong[Rajata] (रजत) — most common technical term; literally "white"
- #strong[Tara] (तार) — "star"; elevated/celestial connotation
- #strong[Rupya] (रूप्य) — "shining," cognate with #emph[rupa] (form,
  beauty)
- #strong[Chandra-loha] (चन्द्र-लोह) — "moon-metal," explicit graha
  correspondence
- #strong[Chandrahasa] (चन्द्रहास) — "smile of the moon"
- #strong[Sweta] (श्वेत) — "white"
- #strong[Soumya] (सौम्य) — "gentle/soothing"; reference to silver’s
  cooling therapeutic effect
- #strong[Rajatadhatu] (रजतधातु) — "silver-metal" (formal)
- #strong[Indu] (इन्दु) — Vedic term, "bright"

The naming conventions emphasize whiteness, brightness, and lunar
quality. Compared to gold’s nomenclature (which emphasizes color, fire,
auspiciousness), silver’s terms emphasize coolness, gentleness, and
reflective quality.

=== 1.3 Graha correspondence
<graha-correspondence>
Silver corresponds to #strong[Chandra] (Moon) in the classical Indian
astrological framework. This correspondence is unanimous across primary
