Friendship pattern of Chandra in BPHS naisargika: - Chandra is friend
of: Surya (Sun), Budha (Mercury) - Chandra is enemy of: none (uniquely
among grahas) - Chandra is neutral to: Mangala (Mars), Guru (Jupiter),
Shukra (Venus), Shani (Saturn)

The #emph[no-enemy] status is significant. Chandra integrates with all
grahas. This is why #strong[silver appears in nearly all sacred alloys]
— panchaloha, ashtadhatu, swarna-tara — and why silver-containing
compounds tend to have broader timing-window favorability than other
dhatu operations.

=== 1.4 Origins and Sources
<origins-and-sources>
Classical Indian silver sources were limited compared to gold and
copper. The major historical sources were: - #strong[Jawar mines
(Rajasthan)] — possibly the world’s earliest large-scale silver mining;
lead-silver smelting evidence from ~400 BCE - #strong[Aravalli ranges
(Rajasthan)] — galena (lead sulfide containing silver) deposits -
#strong[Trade silver] — significant import from Central Asia, Persia,
and (later) the New World via European trade - #strong[Gold-mine
byproduct] — silver naturally occurs alloyed with gold (electrum);
refining gold yielded silver as byproduct

The classical texts acknowledge multiple silver sources without
elaborate origin mythology (unlike gold’s #emph[sahaja swarna]
cosmic-origin narrative). Silver is treated more as workmanlike metal —
useful, valuable, but not ritually transcendent in the way gold is.

