== 2. The Shodhana–Marana Sequence
<the-shodhanamarana-sequence>
=== 2.1 Shodhana (Purification)
<shodhana-purification>
The classical purification protocol for silver follows the same
structure as gold’s quenching sequence per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya]:

#strong[Procedure:] 1. #strong[Taila (sesame oil)] — heat silver foil to
red-hot, quench in oil 7 times 2. #strong[Takra (buttermilk)] — quench 7
times 3. #strong[Go-mutra (cow’s urine)] — quench 7 times 4.
#strong[Aranala (fermented kanji)] — quench 7 times 5.
#strong[Kulattha-kvatha (horse-gram decoction)] — quench 7 times

Total: 35 quenching cycles in 5 different media. Same procedure as gold,
with silver requiring slightly lower red-hot temperature (~600-700°C,
compared to gold’s 700-800°C) due to silver’s lower melting point.

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

The chemistry is largely parallel to gold’s shodhana with one important
difference: silver is more reactive than gold, so the quenching media
interact more vigorously with silver surface: - #strong[Sesame oil]:
organic medium provides reducing atmosphere; sulfur-containing oils
cause some surface sulfide formation (this is actually beneficial — it
forms a thin protective Ag₂S layer that prevents further reaction during
subsequent steps) - #strong[Buttermilk]: lactic acid removes surface
oxides AND any sulfide formed in oil step - #strong[Cow urine]: alkaline
ammoniacal environment removes silver chloride contaminants (silver
halides are sparingly soluble but dissolve in ammonia) - #strong[Kanji]:
acidic, removes residual oxides - #strong[Kulattha]: amino-acid-rich,
final conditioning

The cycling between oxidizing (oil, kanji acidic) and reducing (urine,
kulattha) media works the silver surface through repeated
oxidation-reduction, leaving a clean and microstructurally refined
product.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Standard procedure across licensed
Ayurvedic pharmacies.

=== 2.2 Marana (Incineration)
<marana-incineration>
The path from purified silver to #emph[rajata-bhasma] differs from
gold’s in mechanism, even though the procedure looks similar.

#strong[Procedure (per Bhowmick et al. 2017 and standardized
references):] 1. #emph[Shuddha Rajata] (purified silver) is rolled into
thin foils 2. Equal parts purified mercury and purified sulfur are
triturated to form #emph[kajjali] (HgS-mercury mixture) 3. Silver foils
are sandwiched within kajjali; triturated with #emph[nimbu-swarasa]
(lemon juice) 4. Cakes formed and dried 5. Sealed in
#emph[sharava-samputa] (earthen-plate enclosure) 6. Subjected to
#emph[gajaputa] (large-fire) heating — typical 18-22 cycles for
completion (vs gold’s 14-30)

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

This is where silver diverges from gold. The chemistry that occurs:

#strong[Cycle 1-3 (early cycles)]: Silver foil + Hg + S in citric acid
medium under heat. Silver readily forms amalgam with mercury (Ag-Hg
solid solutions to ~30 wt% Hg). Sulfur reacts with both — forming HgS
and Ag₂S simultaneously. The intermediate compound is a Ag-Hg-S phase,
identified by XRD as #emph[imitrite] (Ag₂Hg₂S), a known mineral.

#strong[Cycle 4-10 (middle cycles)]: Heating drives off mercury (boiling
point 357°C, well below the puta temperature of ~600°C). What’s left is
increasingly Ag₂S, which is thermodynamically stable. The silver does
NOT decompose back to elemental form because Ag₂S is more stable than
separated Ag + S at the temperatures used.

#strong[Cycle 11+ (late cycles)]: Continued heating-cooling cycles
refine the Ag₂S particle size to nanoscale. The final product is
#strong[acanthite] (α-Ag₂S, monoclinic) particles in the 10-100 nm
range.

#strong[The key difference from gold]: - Gold-bhasma: gold + mercury →
amalgam → drive off mercury → pure Au nanoparticles (Au is too noble to
react with sulfur at puta temperatures) - Silver-bhasma: silver +
mercury + sulfur → imitrite intermediate → drive off Hg → Ag₂S
nanoparticles (silver IS reactive enough to bond with sulfur and stay
bonded)

#strong[Modern characterization (peer-reviewed):]

