== 5. Gap Analysis: Tradition vs Modern Interpretation
<gap-analysis-tradition-vs-modern-interpretation>
=== 5.1 Where the tradition is correct
<where-the-tradition-is-correct>
- #strong[Silver bhasma is biologically active] — accurately observed;
  modern studies confirm anti-cancer, antimicrobial, anti-inflammatory
  effects
- #strong[Silver-bhasma should be at very fine particle size] —
  accurately observed; modern characterization confirms 10-100 nm
- #strong[Silver has cooling/calming properties] — supported partially;
  silver compounds have antimicrobial action that reduces inflammation,
  which classically presents as "cooling"
- #strong[Silver is suitable for mental/emotional disorders] — under
  investigation; some preliminary research supports effects on
  neurological conditions, more work needed
- #strong[Silver tarnishes through environmental interaction] — the
  #emph[kanchuka]-equivalent observation for silver; modern chemistry
  confirms surface H₂S reaction
- #strong[The puta procedure produces a stable bhasma form] — accurately
  observed; Ag₂S is thermodynamically stable and doesn’t revert to
  elemental Ag at body temperatures
- #strong[Silver responds to environmental influences more than gold] —
  accurately observed; silver’s electrical conductivity, magnetic
  susceptibility, and chemical reactivity are all higher than gold’s

=== 5.2 Where the tradition’s claims need qualification
<where-the-traditions-claims-need-qualification>
- #strong["Rajata-bhasma is silver"] — partially correct; the bhasma is
  silver-based but specifically silver sulfide (Ag₂S), not metallic
  silver. Therapeutically, this distinction matters less than it would
  seem (Ag₂S has bioactivity in its own right) but is worth being
  precise about
- #strong[Specific clinical efficacy claims (cures epilepsy, etc.)] —
  under-investigated by modern controlled trials
- #strong["Tara-bhasma" of pure metallic silver] — would require
  different chemistry than the kajjali-based marana; possibly achievable
  with non-sulfur-containing co-substances but not typical of classical
  recipes
